// Added Nov-14-2025
//
.SINEW YARN.
Sinew yarn is a strong, durable thread made from the fibrous tendons of animals.
It is commonly used for sewing leather and fur, as well as for various crafting and binding tasks, such as attaching arrowheads.
However, sinew yarn cannot be used for fishing tackle, as it weakens and stretches when wet.<br>
<br>
To make sinew yarn, you first need animal tendons. These can be harvested from butchered animals of relatively large size.
Leg tendons of large mammals, such as elk and reindeer, are preferred, but tendons from smaller animals can also be used,
though they yield less useful sinew. Tendons are processed into sinew yarn using your TEXTILECRAFT skill through the following
stages of work:<br>
<br>
    TENDON DRYING - First of all, fresh tendons need to be dried. They are spread out flat or hung in a dry, preferably warm place until stiff. This takes a day or two.<br>
<br>
    SEPARATE SINEW FIBRE - Next, the dried tendon is pounded with a club or similar tool on a hard surface
until the sinew fibres begin to separate. The fluffy, thin fibres are then pulled apart and collected by hand into small bundles.<br>
<br>
    SINEW YARN - Finally, the obtained sinew fibres can be twisted together to form sinew yarn. 
This is done entirely by hand, as a spindle cannot be used. The fibres are occasionally moistened in the mouth to make them easier
to handle and join as the yarn is being made.
***

// added Mar-24-2021
// updated Nov-14-2025
//
.TEXTILECRAFT.TEXTILE PLANTS.
Textilecraft involves extracting plant or sinew fibres and spinning them into yarn.
Flax, hemp, and nettle are the plants from which plant fibres can be obtained. Sinew yarn is made from animal tendons.
A character's expertise in textilecraft affects the required working times and the amount of usable fibre or yarn produced.
Processing plant fibres into yarn involves several stages of work as follows:<br> 
<br>
 RETTING - After harvesting, the fibres must be separated from the plant stalks. 
This process, called retting, involves soaking the plants in water for several days. 
The duration depends on water temperature, with warmer water speeding up retting. 
Retting does not occur in very cold water, so it cannot be done in winter.<br>
<br>
 DRYING - Once retting is complete, the plant stalks are removed from the water and left to dry outdoors in loose bundles.
They must dry fully to be easily crushed in the next work phase of extracting the fibre. 
Drying takes about a week, after which the dried stalks can be stored for later or processed for fibre as needed.<br>
<br>
 EXTRACT FIBRE - The fibres can now be extracted from the stalks. This is done by breaking the dried, retted stalks so the woody core
shatters and falls away, leaving the loose fibres free. The stalks are beaten, for example with a club on a solid surface. 
It's hard work, usually done by several people together. 
There is an old custom of arranging village work parties for fibre processing with neighbouring households. 
Obtained bundles of fibre are then scraped free of remaining coarse parts, either with tools at hand or by rubbing and whacking
them against a hard surface.<br>
    Nettle fibres can be extracted directly by hand from the stalks, even without retting. 
Simply use the extract option directly on freshly harvested nettles.<br>
<br>
 SPIN YARN - Finally, the obtained bundles of fibre can be spun into yarn.
Spinning can be done either by hand, which is slower, or using a spindle.<br>
<br>
To make sinew yarn, a spindle cannot be used; it is made entirely by hand and naturally involves different work stages.
<br>
